          Updated October 6, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS
        </div>
        <a href="#verdict" class="btn-ghost">The Verdict</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        We research every product on this page independently. If you buy through our links, we may earn a
        commission at no cost to you. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Learn more about how we review.</a>
      </p>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          "StairMaster or treadmill?" is one of the most common arguments in any gym, and it
          usually generates more heat than light because the two sides are answering different
          questions. One is asking which machine works the legs harder for twenty minutes; the other
          is asking which machine they could live with for ten years.
        </p>

        <aside class="ed-note not-prose">
          <span class="ed-note-k">What we checked</span>
          <strong class="text-gray-900">On the figures, and on health.</strong> Sizes, weights and
          prices below are broad typical ranges for each category, not measurements of particular
          models, and they vary considerably — check the manufacturer's listing before planning
          around any of them. We review equipment and do not give medical advice; if you have a
          joint problem, a heart condition or are returning from injury, ask a clinician or
          physiotherapist which kind of exercise suits you.
        </aside>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="In this guide">
          <h2>In this guide</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#verdict">Is a StairMaster Better Than a Treadmill? The Verdict</a></li>
            <li><a href="#machine-types">What Counts as a Stair Climber</a></li>
            <li><a href="#muscles">Muscles Worked</a></li>
            <li><a href="#calories">Calorie Burn: Closer Than the Arguments Suggest</a></li>
            <li><a href="#impact-knees">Impact, Joints and Knees</a></li>
            <li><a href="#skill-balance">Skill, Balance and Who Finds Each Easy</a></li>
            <li><a href="#home-suitability">Owning One at Home: Where the Stair Climber Struggles</a></li>
            <li><a href="#versatility">Versatility: The Treadmill’s Biggest Advantage</a></li>
            <li><a href="#incline-middle-ground">The Middle Ground: an Incline Treadmill</a></li>
            <li><a href="#who-should-pick">Who Should Pick Which</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Is a StairMaster Better Than a Treadmill? The Verdict</h2>
<p>
            <strong>A stair climber is not better than a treadmill overall, but it is better at one
            specific thing:</strong> delivering a hard, low-impact, glute- and quad-heavy workout in a
            short time. A treadmill is better at almost everything else — it handles walking,
            running and incline work, suits a much wider range of fitness levels, takes up less
            vertical space, costs less to buy for home use and is easier to live with. For most
            people buying a machine for home, the treadmill is the right choice. For people with
            access to a gym, using both is better than arguing about them.
          </p>
          <p>
            A note on the name. StairMaster is a brand, and the best-known one, so "StairMaster" has
            become the everyday word for a stair climber in the way "Hoover" once meant any vacuum.
            On this page "stair climber" means the machine type, chiefly the revolving-staircase
            design you see in gyms. Smaller pedal-type steppers are a different machine and come up
            separately below.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are weighing a treadmill against other <a href="/best-cardio-machines-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>cardio machines</a> too, our <a href="/treadmill-vs-elliptical/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill vs elliptical comparison</a> covers the other half of the usual gym debate.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="machine-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What Counts as a Stair Climber</h2>
<p>
            Three quite different machines get called stair climbers, and the comparison depends on
            which one you mean.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Revolving stair climbers</strong>, sometimes called stepmills, are a motorised
            loop of real steps that rotates downward while you climb. You set a step rate and keep up
            with it. This is the machine most people picture when they say StairMaster, and it is the
            one this comparison is mostly about.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Pedal steppers</strong> have two foot plates that move up and down on resistance.
            The movement is shorter and less like real stairs, they are much smaller and cheaper, and
            compact versions are sold for home use. They are a lighter workout than a revolving
            climber for most people.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Mini steppers</strong> are small, often under-desk or floor units with a short
            stroke. They are closer to fidgeting with resistance than to climbing stairs and are not
            a real alternative to a treadmill. Our <a href="/stepper-buying-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">stepper buying guide</a> compares mini, twist and full-size steppers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="muscles" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Muscles Worked</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Stair climber:</strong> every step lifts your whole body weight upward from a
            bent knee and hip, which loads the glutes and quadriceps heavily, with substantial work
            for the calves and hamstrings. This is the main reason people who want stronger, more
            developed glutes favour it. The effort is concentrated and you feel it quickly.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Treadmill:</strong> walking and running on the flat use the whole leg in a more
            even, cyclical way — calves, hamstrings, quads and glutes all contribute, with no single
            group taking the brunt. Running adds more calf and hip work and more demand on the
            stabilising muscles.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Treadmill on an incline:</strong> raising the deck shifts the work toward the
            posterior chain — glutes, hamstrings and calves — and makes each step more of an upward
            push. It does not reproduce the deep knee bend of climbing a step, but it closes a large
            part of the gap. That is the logic behind the popular <a href="/12-3-30-treadmill-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">12-3-30 workout</a>,
            which is essentially stair-climber-style effort delivered on a treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            Neither machine builds much upper-body strength, and neither is a substitute for strength
            training if muscle is the goal. Both are cardio machines with a different lower-body
            emphasis.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="calories" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Calorie Burn: Closer Than the Arguments Suggest</h2>
<p>
            The popular claim is that stair climbers burn far more calories than treadmills. The
            more accurate version is that stair climbing is very hard at low speeds, because you are
            lifting your body weight vertically with every step, so it gets most people to a high
            effort level faster than flat walking does.
          </p>
          <p>
            Matched for effort, the difference largely disappears. Calories burned are driven mainly
            by how hard you work, for how long, and how much you weigh. A steady stair-climber session
            and a jog or a steep incline walk at the same perceived effort and heart rate land in the
            same broad range. A gentle flat walk burns much less than either, but that is a
            comparison of intensities rather than machines.
          </p>
          <p>
            Be sceptical of the calorie figures on any console. They are estimates built from
            averages and tend to run optimistic, and the figures on stair climbers and treadmills are
            not necessarily calculated the same way, so comparing one machine's display with
            another's tells you little. Our explainer on whether <a href="/are-treadmill-calories-accurate/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill calorie counts are accurate</a> covers the estimate itself, and we go into what it means for weight in our guide to <a href="/treadmill-workout-for-weight-loss/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill workouts for weight loss</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical point is that the machine you will use consistently, at a meaningful
            effort, burns more over a year than the one that is theoretically more efficient but that
            you avoid.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="impact-knees" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Impact, Joints and Knees</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Impact.</strong> A stair climber is low impact: your foot lands on a step rather
            than striking the ground at speed, and there is no flight phase. Treadmill walking is also
            low impact. Treadmill running is the highest-impact option here, though a cushioned deck
            softens it compared with pavement.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Knees.</strong> Low impact is not the same as low knee load. Climbing stairs puts
            the knee through a deep bend under body weight on every step, which loads the front of the
            knee considerably. Some people with knee pain find stairs aggravating for exactly this
            reason, while others tolerate them well; it is individual. We look at the treadmill side
            of that question in <a href="/are-treadmills-bad-for-your-knees/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">are treadmills bad for your knees</a>. Walking on a treadmill, flat or
            at a modest incline, keeps the knee straighter through each step.
          </p>
          <p>
            If knees are the deciding factor, the comparison is less "stair climber versus treadmill"
            than "which movement does your knee tolerate", and that is a question for a clinician or
            physiotherapist. Our guide to <a href="/best-treadmills-for-bad-knees/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for bad knees</a> covers the machine features — cushioning, low starting speed, long rails — that make a
            treadmill easier on joints.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skill-balance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Skill, Balance and Who Finds Each Easy</h2>
<p>
            A treadmill asks almost nothing of you beyond walking, which is why it suits beginners,
            older users and people returning to exercise. You can start at a slow stroll and add speed
            or incline in small increments, and long handrails provide reassurance.
          </p>
          <p>
            A revolving stair climber is harder to start on. Even the slowest step rate is a real
            workout for an unconditioned person, the steps keep coming whether or not you are ready,
            and the movement takes a little coordination. Leaning on the rails makes it much easier,
            which is why you often see people draped over the handles — and why the workout they are
            getting is far lighter than it looks.
          </p>
          <p>
            Both machines have emergency stops and safety features, and both deserve respect. Learn
            where the stop is before you start, keep a light grip only, and step off carefully
            rather than mid-stride.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="home-suitability" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Owning One at Home: Where the Stair Climber Struggles</h2>
<p>
            This is where the comparison becomes lopsided for most households.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Height and ceilings.</strong> A revolving stair climber is tall — commonly well
            over six feet — and you stand on a step that lifts you some way off the floor, then rise
            further with each step. In a room with a standard or <a href="/best-treadmills-for-low-ceilings/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>low ceiling</a>, and especially in a
            basement, head clearance can rule one out. Measure your ceiling and compare it with the
            machine's height plus your own height plus the step rise before buying.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Weight.</strong> Commercial revolving climbers commonly weigh several hundred
            pounds, which matters for upstairs rooms, delivery and moving it again later.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Price.</strong> Revolving stair climbers are largely commercial products, and
            prices for a new one typically run to several thousand dollars — more than a good home
            treadmill. Pedal steppers are far cheaper, but they are also a lighter workout.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Footprint.</strong> A stair climber's floor footprint can be comparable to or
            smaller than a treadmill's, so floor space is not usually the issue; height, weight and
            cost are. Treadmills also fold, in many cases. Our <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill dimensions guide</a> covers the space a treadmill really needs, including the clearance behind it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="versatility" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Versatility: The Treadmill’s Biggest Advantage</h2>
<p>
            A treadmill does several jobs. It is a walking machine for easy days and recovery, a
            running machine for training, an incline machine for low-impact hard work, and a platform
            for intervals, steady sessions and long walks with a podcast. A household with different
            users — one runner, one walker — can share it.
          </p>
          <p>
            A stair climber does one job very well. It is a superb tool for short, hard, low-impact
            sessions, and it is useful as a change of stimulus for runners. But it is not a running
            machine, it is not a pleasant easy-day machine for most people, and long sessions on it
            are a serious undertaking.
          </p>
          <p>
            For a gym user, that specialisation is fine because the gym has both. For a single
            machine at home, versatility usually wins, because the best home machine is the one that
            still fits your training when your goals change.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="incline-middle-ground" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Middle Ground: an Incline Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            If what attracts you to a stair climber is hard, low-impact, glute-focused work, an
            incline treadmill delivers much of that without giving up walking and running. Walking
            briskly at a steep incline raises heart rate to stair-climber territory for many people
            without the impact of running, and it shifts the work toward the glutes and hamstrings.
          </p>
          <p>
            Most home treadmills offer incline up to somewhere around 10 to 15%, and some incline
            trainers go considerably steeper — check the specification of any particular model. Our <a href="/best-incline-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best incline treadmills</a> guide covers the machines that do this well, and <a href="/treadmill-incline-percent-vs-degrees/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">incline percent vs degrees</a> explains what those numbers mean as an actual slope.
          </p>
          <p>
            What an incline treadmill does not do is reproduce the deep knee bend of a stair. If that
            specific movement is what you are after — for sport, for hiking preparation with lots of
            steps, or simply because you enjoy it — a gym stair climber remains the better tool.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-pick" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Should Pick Which</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Choose a treadmill if</strong> you are buying one machine for home, you want to
            walk, run or both, several people will use it, you are a beginner or returning to
            exercise, you have limited ceiling height, or you want the most flexible machine for the
            money. An incline-capable model gives you the stair-climber-style option too.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Choose a stair climber if</strong> you have gym access and want a short, hard,
            low-impact session with a strong glute and quad emphasis, you are a runner looking for a
            non-impact way to add hard work, or you are training for something that involves a lot of
            climbing. For home use, it makes sense only if you have the ceiling height, the floor
            strength and the budget, and you know you will use it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Use both if you can.</strong> In a gym, alternating them across the week gives
            different stimuli and keeps training interesting, which is worth more than the marginal
            difference between them.
          </p>
          <p>
            If a treadmill is the answer, our <a href="/treadmill-buying-guide-2026/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill buying guide</a> explains which specifications matter for your kind of use.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes When Choosing Between Them</h2>
          <p>Most bad decisions here come from comparing the machines at different intensities, or forgetting where the machine will actually live.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Comparing a hard stair session with an easy walk</h3>
              <p>Most "stair climbers burn more" claims compare a hard stair-climber session with a gentle flat treadmill walk. At matched effort the difference shrinks to very little. Compare the machines at the same intensity, or you are really comparing intensities.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Leaning on the handrails</h3>
              <p>Hanging on to the rails of a stair climber takes a large share of your body weight off your legs, which is why it looks hard and feels easy. The same applies to holding a treadmill’s rails on a steep incline. A light touch for balance is fine; supporting your weight undoes the workout.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Forgetting the ceiling</h3>
              <p>A revolving stair climber is tall, you stand on a raised step, and you rise further with each step. People measure floor space and forget head clearance, which is the dimension that rules most of these machines out of basements and low-ceilinged rooms.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Assuming low impact means easy on the knees</h3>
              <p>Stair climbing has very little impact but a lot of knee bend under load. For some people with knee problems that is the worse combination, not the better one. Low impact and low knee load are different things, and a physiotherapist can tell you which matters for you.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Neither machine is better overall.</strong> A stair climber
            (StairMaster is the best-known brand) gives a short, hard, low-impact, glute-heavy
            workout. A treadmill is more versatile, suits more people, and is far easier to own —
            stair climbers are tall, heavy and expensive.
          </p>
          <p>
            For most home buyers, the answer is a treadmill with a good incline range, which covers
            walking, running and much of what the stair climber offers. Start with our <a href="/best-incline-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">best incline treadmills</a> guide, and use a stair climber at the gym when you want the real thing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <aside class="ed-more not-prose">
          <div class="ed-more-k">Keep reading</div>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="/treadmill-vs-elliptical/">Treadmill vs Elliptical</a></li>
            <li><a href="/best-incline-treadmills/">Best Incline Treadmills</a></li>
          </ul>
        </aside>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Is StairMaster better than treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not overall. A stair climber (StairMaster is the best-known brand) is better for short, hard, low-impact sessions that work the glutes and quads intensely. A treadmill is more versatile, suits more fitness levels and is far more practical to own at home. For most home buyers a treadmill, ideally with incline, is the better choice; in a gym, using both is best.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is StairMaster better than treadmill for weight loss?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not inherently. Calorie burn is driven mainly by effort, duration and body weight. A stair climber gets most people to a high effort quickly, but a jog or a steep incline walk at the same effort burns a similar amount. The machine you use consistently matters far more, and console calorie figures are optimistic estimates.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Does a stair climber work the glutes more than a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Generally yes. Each step lifts your body weight from a bent hip and knee, which loads the glutes and quads heavily. Flat treadmill walking spreads the work more evenly across the leg. Walking on a steep treadmill incline shifts much more of the work to the glutes and hamstrings and closes a large part of the gap.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a stair climber or treadmill better for bad knees?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It depends on the knee. A stair climber is low impact but bends the knee deeply under load on every step, which some people with knee pain find aggravating. Treadmill walking keeps the knee straighter and is also low impact. Which suits you is individual, so ask a clinician or physiotherapist.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can an incline treadmill replace a stair climber?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most purposes, largely yes. Brisk walking at a steep incline raises heart rate substantially with low impact and shifts work toward the glutes and hamstrings. It does not reproduce the deep knee bend of climbing steps, so if that specific movement matters for your sport or training, a stair climber is still the better tool.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you put a stair climber in your home?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              You can, but check three things first. Revolving stair climbers are tall, commonly well over six feet, and you rise further as you step, so ceiling height can rule one out. They are also heavy, often several hundred pounds, and typically cost more than a good home treadmill. Pedal steppers are smaller and cheaper but a lighter workout.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

